- P-54 **`QQ`'s `ceiling` and `truncate` keep `ZZ`, their declared result type, and raise an error at +∞, −∞ and 0/0.** The specification's paragraph on these methods said that they return the argument itself at those three values (`Specification/basic-lib/numbers.tex`, "Rational Numbers"). That paragraph is revised. `floor`, `round` and the brackets ⌊x⌋ and ⌈x⌉ are built on `ceiling` and `truncate`, so they raise too. Why: the declared integer result and other languages win over the paragraph: Python's `math.floor(inf)`, for one, raises. The library raises `DivisionByZero`, as the revised paragraph says. Later, a rung after the switch-over makes `QQ` follow the float ruling (P-68, on rounding the float types). Then `floor` and `ceiling` return `QQ` and pass the infinities through, and the brackets, `round` and `truncate` give `ZZ`.
  by: the scrape; note: status and planning references deleted, at the curator's word of 2026-10-22

- P-56 **The open range `(:)` keeps its wildcard type, and its five cutting methods fail.** `(:)` is the range of every index, as in `a[:]`. It is the object `TrivialOpenRange`, which stays an `OpenRange[\Any\]`: the team used `Any` as a wildcard for any index type. Its methods `truncL`, `truncR`, `every`, `imposeStride` and `atMost` cut a range, so each must answer a range over `Any`. The library's range operators build ranges only over `ZZ32` and its pairs and triples, and a range over `ZZ32` is not a range over `Any`. So no body that answers a range passes the compiled checker. Each of the five is a `fail` with a message, the team's own device for a body that cannot answer its declared type. Nothing in the tree calls them on `(:)` but the five tests `ProjectFortress/tests/TrivialOpenRange*Stop.fss`, which pin the `fail`. The other way, `(:)` as a range over `ZZ32`, is not taken. Why: a probe under walk tried that way on small programs. More of them stopped with an error where they had printed a result than printed a result where they had stopped (`reviews/ranges-and-index-types.md`, section 5).
  by: the scrape; note: status and planning references deleted, at the curator's word of 2026-10-22

- P-57 **`LexicographicReduction.isLeftZero` takes `TotalComparison`, so the team's answers are reached.** `LexicographicReduction` is the library's reduction behind `BIG LEXICO`, which combines comparisons in dictionary order. `isLeftZero(c)` says whether `c LEXICO x` is `c` for every `x`. The team declared `isLeftZero(_:EqualTo)` as `false` and `isLeftZero(_:Comparison)` as `true`. `TotalComparison`, whose values are `LessThan`, `EqualTo` and `GreaterThan`, lies below `Comparison`. The object also inherits `ReductionWithZeroes.isLeftZero` at `TotalComparison`, which answers `false`. That declaration is more specific, so the team's `true` was never reached. The `true` overload is now declared over `TotalComparison`, so it replaces the inherited one: `LessThan` and `GreaterThan` answer `true`, and `EqualTo` `false`. Why: the curator wants the compiled checker's error count on the interpreter's library at zero. This change removed the last of the count's errors in an api file. Other errors kept the count above zero (F-53 holds the count).
  by: the scrape; note: status and planning references deleted, at the curator's word of 2026-10-22

- P-58 **Every native binding is a `builtinPrimitive` call naming a static helper.** A native binding is a library body that is exactly `builtinPrimitive("…")`, the library's own form, and it keeps that form. Its string is to name a static Java method in `nativeHelpers/`, the compiled path's helpers, such as `"com.sun.fortress.nativeHelpers.simpleIntArith.intLT"`, in place of a glue class of walk, which the compiled path cannot use (F-32). Walk is to load the named helper through a loader beside its glue classes. At the parse, before the checker, the compiled path is to rewrite each such body into a synthetic `import java` of the helper and a call, and to refuse a string that names no helper. The bindings move onto helpers rung by rung, and the compiled checker sees each rung's bindings as that rung lands, not all at once at the switch-over. The other form, `import java` declarations written in the library itself, is not taken. Every binding still names a glue class, and neither the loader nor the rewrite exists. The first natives rung builds them, test first, and the later rungs move the bindings.
  by: the scrape; note: status and planning references deleted, at the curator's word of 2026-10-22

- P-59 **Walk chooses a coercion by the run-time value until it runs the checker, after the switch-over.** Walk has no static types. So where a coercion is needed, walk chooses it by the value's run-time type. The specification and the compiled path choose it by the static type. The curator accepts this as the interpreter's limit, and walk is not changed for it before the switch-over. A callout in the coercion chapter (`Specification/basic/conversions-coercions.tex`) states the limit. Two expected-failure tests record it: `XXXCoercionStaticRungC`, the chapter's own printed example, and `XXXCoercionStaticNarrowRungC`. After the switch-over, once walk runs the checker on its programs (P-12 orders the two steps), it uses the static coercions that the checker inserts. MicroGPT's static types are concrete, each the type of the value itself and not a trait above it, so on microGPT walk's choice and the static choice agree.
  by: the scrape; note: status and planning references deleted, at the curator's word of 2026-10-22

- P-62 **The `covariant` keyword is future work, off the path to microGPT.** The keyword, with `contravariant`, is the team's 2012 declaration-site variance: under `trait C[\covariant S\]`, a `C[\Dog\]` is a `C[\Animal\]`, as with Scala's `+S`. No library declaration and no line of microGPT uses it. Row 404 records the hole: the compiled checker accepts variance, but the compiled run dies with `IncompatibleClassChangeError`, and walk refuses the program. The future work is declaration-site variance, carried to run time on both paths. When it lands, a rung tries to bring back the specification's sign-refined number types as covariant static parameters. P-80 keeps their design word for word in the specification, marked superseded.
  by: the scrape; note: status and planning references deleted, at the curator's word of 2026-10-22

- P-147 **An object defines an inherited abstract method when its bodies of that name at narrower parameter types together cover the abstract method's parameter types.** That is, each value of the abstract method's parameter types is a value of one body's parameter types. A `comprises` clause counts as the compiled checker reads it, which is P-60's reading: a closed trait is covered by its listed types. A body at the abstract method's own parameter types is the plain case. This is the team's rule of 2012, in Steele's abstract-method check of the compiled checker and in the Covered rule of the Welterweight calculus (`Papers/Welterweight/`). So the team's `Library/Pairs.fss` and `ProjectFortress/tests/FileConversion.fss` stay valid: an object in each defines an inherited abstract operator once for each type that a `comprises` clause lists. Walk applies the same rule at load, and its earlier allowance of any one narrower body goes. The traits chapter's sentence, which admitted no narrower body, is revised in the specification's revision form (P-96). Row 725: the acceptance of some valid objects whose methods have static parameters of their own, which the checker and walk (row 728) refuse. Row 665: walk's check of generic objects, which its load check skips.
  by: the scrape; note: status and planning references deleted, at the curator's word of 2026-10-22; each row pointer kept, built from the deleted sentence's own words

- P-149 **An object that inherits an abstract functional method from one trait, and a body for it from a sibling trait, is refused unless it declares the method itself.** In `object O extends { A, B }`, where `A` declares `pick(self)` abstract and `B` gives `pick(self)` a body, `O` must declare its own `pick`. Scala's way, where the sibling trait's body implements the abstract method, is not taken. The compiled checker refuses such an object, as the specification's text and the type group's Welterweight calculus (`Papers/Welterweight/`) require. Walk already refuses it at load, by the Meet Rule for Functional Methods. A trait that declares one method both abstract and with a body, at the same parameter types, is refused too. Why: the revival honors the designers' intent. It changes Fortress's nature only to reconcile a conflict between the earlier and the later designers' choices, and then looks to other languages for how to do it. Here there is no such conflict, and Fortress is not bent to Scala's way for a hypothetical convenience.
  by: the scrape; note: status and planning references deleted, at the curator's word of 2026-10-22

- P-151 **Only the `where`-clause forms that the team wrote down are built: conditional extension, a function's or a method's condition, and `AnyList`'s `comprises` clause.** A `where` clause states conditions on static parameters, and may introduce static variables of its own, as `where [\E\]` does. A trait or object that extends a type under a `where` condition is that type only where the condition holds, and its members are checked with the condition holding (conditional extension). A `where` condition on a function's or a method's own static parameters bounds them. The team's commented-out clause `AnyList comprises List[\E\] where [\E\]` becomes live, which clears the type error of `AnyList`'s join `||`; its other operations, `addLeft` and `addRight`, move into `List`. `SUM`'s fusion with `MAX` and `MIN`, the library's fast path for the maximum or the minimum of sums, stays. A method's `where` condition clears four of the fusion's six type errors, and the team's commented-out generic `distribute`, after a measurement, the other two (F-242: the errors and how each is cleared). Why: a feature with little support in the team's work bends the language beyond what the curator can review. He has such a feature built where the team left enough design intent for it, or where it is on the critical path to running fast compiled. Climb batch 17 kept `addLeft` and `addRight` in `AnyList` instead, respelled under the live clause, against the option he chose. Row 773: the members scoped to the condition, since a member checked under it stays callable where the condition fails. Row 774: walk's half of conditional extension. Row 701: the code generator's half.
  by: the scrape; note: status and planning references deleted, at the curator's word of 2026-10-22; each row pointer kept, built from the deleted sentence's own words

- P-152 **Walk and the compiled checker read the exclusion of two generic declarations over their whole parameter types, by existential reduction.** With static parameters, the parameter types of two declarations can exclude each other as a whole, even where no single parameter excludes its counterpart. In `o[\T\](x: P[\T\], b: Box[\T\])` beside `o[\T\](x: Q[\T\], b: Box[\T\])`, where `P[\T\]` excludes `Q[\T\]` only at the same `T`, neither parameter excludes alone. But no value is a `Box` of two types, so `b` fixes the same `T` for both declarations, and no argument fits both. A generic trait in `Box`'s place fixes `T` in the same way. Both paths read such a pair as excluding, and judge it on that reading by the overloading rules (P-45: the 2011 paper's No Duplicates, Meet and Return Type rules). Why: the type group's 2011 paper decides exclusion between parameter types with static parameters by existential reduction (`Papers/Types/exc-reduction.tick`; P-26, the type group's later word outweighs the specification's early text), as the revised specification states (`Specification/advanced/overloading.tex`, "Declarations with Static Parameters"). Walk refuses such a pair at load where one declaration has a `where` clause, which the checker accepts (row 787); and walk reads an object type over a static parameter, such as `Box[\T\]`, as excluding a type that it equals at some instance, such as `Box[\ZZ32\]`, so that it loads a pair that the checker refuses (row 788).
